Z||000000||Pheagey||10-15-2001||10:25 AM||||Are replacment cylinoids any stronger than the stock one? I ask 'cause I busted my stock one turning the bleed screw to tiet with only one hand. If it helps I got the replacment from discount Auto Parts.<P>thanks<P>  <IMG SRC="Http://www.LithiumEffects.com/car.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Pheagey (edited 10-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Voytek||10-15-2001||03:10 PM||||I assume you mean the slave cylinder - the aftermarket ones aren't any better than original.  I've done the same thing on mine -tightened the bleed screw too much and snapped off the whole bleed screw/threaded socket.  Remember this when you buy a new one - it really doesn't take much force to seal the bleed screw.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||samoht||10-15-2001||05:04 PM||||i bought one from napa..  they make the joint that attaches the bleeder thicker<BR> ]  ]         ]  ]<BR> ]  ]         ]  ]<BR> \  /         ]  ]<BR>--  ------   -   ---------<BR>__________   _____________<BR>Stock          Napa<BR> <BR>they dont have to be very tight, about 1/4 turn past hand tight.. Do a search on bleeding too...  Gravity worked the best for me||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fierowrecker||10-15-2001||08:15 PM||||I also snapped mine off and got a NAPA replacement, but mine was CAST!!!<BR>Very strong, and it works well...<BR><P>------------------<BR>crash... The Fierowrecker<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://fiero.nl/uploads/crash_sig.jpg">        <IMG SRC="http://fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Pheagey||10-15-2001||09:09 PM||||   Hey thanks ya'll. The one i got from Discount auto was the GM replacment. Very strong, nice black paint job on it to boot. I don't think this one will break for a while.<BR>   After one is changed is it standard to have to bleed it more than one? I ask this 'cause it now shift but down shift kinda hard. and I bleed it at work (after clocking out) and got more air out of it...<BR>||||reg||1
